    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d527">

                <pb facs="0599" n="501" sameAs="frus:frus1932v04/pg501"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.1111 Baker, Charles/83: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Consul General at
                        Shanghai (Cunningham)</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-04-25T17:00:00-04:00">April 25, 1932—5 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>154. For the Minister. Your April 23, noon, and Department’s 151, April 23, 2
                    p.m. Department desires that you continue your supervision of the efforts being
                    made to obtain release of Captain Baker but suggests advisability of your
                    instructing Peck to give the case his special attention by following closely all
                    developments and constantly keeping the matter before the ranking officials at
                    Nanking.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Castle</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
